From: android-kitkat-tls12 <android-kitkat-tls12@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/30] Port of d745755, b221fc9, 342097d, 966ae8a, f67d784:
 SNI by default

Send Server Name Indication for every client socket that has a
usable host name, as Android 6.0 does, so that Apache HTTP and plain
SSLSocketFactory users reach SNI-only virtual hosts with the right
certificate. Before, only callers of setHostname() (okhttp,
SSLCertificateSocketFactory) sent SNI.

The 6.0 commits change SSLParametersImpl and Platform of the
standalone tree; the 4.4 OpenSSLSocketImpl keeps its SNI settings
itself, so the behaviour is ported there:

- the (String host, ...) constructors keep the host in a new field
  peerHostname (4.4 passed it to super() only);
- getSniHostname(): an explicit setHostname() wins (null turns SNI
  off, 966ae8a's useSni); otherwise, unless jsse.enableSNIExtension is
  "false" (default "true", read at each handshake; any other value
  throws, 966ae8a/b221fc9/d745755), the host the socket was created
  with or the wrapped socket's host. Never getPeerHostName() or
  getInetAddress().getHostName(), which can do a reverse DNS lookup;
- SSL_set_tlsext_host_name only for names that
  AddressUtils.isValidSniHostname() accepts: a dot, not an IP literal
  (342097d; RFC 6066 section 3); this also applies to setHostname();
- AddressUtils (342097d + the regex matcher of f67d784) and its test,
  Platform.isLiteralIpAddress() = InetAddress.isNumeric() (no lookup),
  plus cases for the literal forms that contain a dot.

The session cache key (getPeerHostName()) and the host passed to the
trust manager (wrappedHost) are unchanged.

Android 4.3 port (android-4.3_r3) of patch 0013 of the 4.4 series. Paths
and names: libcore/crypto of 4.4 (org.conscrypt,
org_conscrypt_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacrypto) is libcore/luni on 4.3
(org.apache.harmony.xnet.provider.jsse,
org_apache_harmony_xnet_provider_jsse_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacore).
4.3 has no Platform class in its JSSE package: the hunk that adds
Platform.isLiteralIpAddress() becomes a new minimal Platform class with
only that method (InetAddress.isNumeric(), no lookup), so AddressUtils
stays as in 4.4. getSniHostname() and isSniEnabledByDefault() are
inserted before getPeerHostName() (4.3 context), and their Javadoc names
the 4.3 socket.
